\paragraph{Implications for router design.}
The dominance of death avoidance in routing signal (DAF $= 90.4\%$ at default $P{=}100$) reframes what a modality router is learning. Rather than discovering efficiency-quality tradeoffs across reasoning strategies, the router primarily learns to predict which states lead to catastrophic failure under internal reasoning. This aligns the routing problem more closely with \emph{safety critics} in safe reinforcement learning~\citep{garcia2015comprehensive} than with the cost-quality optimization framing adopted by existing routing work~\citep{cogrouter-2026, ares-2026, mtrouter-2026}. The practical implication is direct: if death avoidance accounts for the vast majority of routing value, then a lightweight death predictor---trained on state features correlated with hallucination-induced failure---may capture most of the router's benefit without the overhead of full counterfactual labeling. That the oracle outperforms always-deterministic by only 2.89 points further suggests that the residual, non-death routing signal may not justify the complexity of learning a full routing policy.

\paragraph{Label imbalance as a core finding.}
The extreme label distribution---86 deterministic-preferred, 9 internal-preferred, and 150 no-preference episodes---is not an artifact to be corrected but a central result. The 61.2\% no-preference rate indicates that for most episodes, modality choice is irrelevant: both strategies succeed or both fail. Among episodes where modality matters, deterministic is preferred at a 9.6:1 ratio, driven almost entirely by death asymmetry. This explains why always-deterministic is a strong baseline: the router's marginal value is confined to identifying the ${\sim}$4\% of episodes where internal reasoning is genuinely superior. The router training results (\S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}) illustrate why learned routing adds no value in this regime. Step-level balanced accuracy ($54.1 \pm 5.4\%$) exceeds chance, indicating the router captures some signal from same-episode context, but episode-level accuracy ($47.1 \pm 2.2\%$) falls below chance on held-out episodes---worse than the trivial always-deterministic baseline (50.0\%). Four independent ablations confirm this is structural rather than data-limited: (i)~$32{\times}$ data scaling (25\%--800\% of original) yields no episode-level improvement (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}); (ii)~scaling router capacity from 0.6B to 4B shows no gain (\S\ref{sec:ablations}); (iii)~a task-type majority-vote baseline equals always-deterministic, since all 11 task types are deterministic-majority; and (iv)~per-task-type evaluation breakdown reveals that 7 of 11 task types have all-deterministic labels, and routing signal does not transfer across the remaining 4 (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}). The episode-level routing problem is trivially solved by always choosing deterministic: with DAF $= 90.4\%$, the routing signal that exists is overwhelmingly ``avoid death,'' and avoiding death means choosing deterministic computation for every episode. Recent concurrent work on tool-use calibration reaches compatible conclusions from the opposite direction---that LLMs may already possess implicit routing signals~\citep{when2tool-2026} but that external tool invocation carries non-trivial costs~\citep{tool-use-tax-2026}, reinforcing the case for conservative routing defaults.

\paragraph{Penalty sensitivity and generalization.}
The correlation between death-rate differential and performance gap is specific to catastrophic death (score $= -100$); broader failure definitions dissolve the correlation because non-death episodes show near-zero performance differential between deterministic and internal strategies ($\Delta_{\text{nd}} = 2.84$). Defining ``failure'' as any non-positive score yields $r = -0.13$ ($p = 0.70$), while the correlation emerges only at penalty magnitudes $P \geq 50$ ($r = 0.86$, $p = 0.0006$) and reaches $r = 0.97$ at the default $P{=}100$ (Appendix~\ref{app:penalty}). This sharpens our central finding: death is not merely the dominant component of routing signal---it is effectively the \emph{only} component that generates differential routing signal in ScienceWorld. In domains without catastrophic failure costs, non-death routing signals (e.g., reasoning quality differences on surviving episodes) would need to provide the entirety of routing value---and our data suggests these differences are near-zero ($\Delta_{\text{nd}} / \Delta_{\text{full}} = 9.6\%$ at $P{=}100$). This connects to reward shaping more broadly: the relative magnitude of catastrophic versus incremental penalties determines whether any differential routing signal exists at all.

Our findings may also be capability-dependent. The 42.9\% internal-reasoning death rate reflects the failure mode of a 7B-parameter model prone to state hallucination. Larger models with lower hallucination rates would exhibit smaller death-rate differentials, potentially reducing DAF and revealing routing signal currently masked by catastrophic failures. Whether non-death routing signal \emph{emerges} as model capability increases---or remains negligible---is an open question with direct implications for the modality routing agenda.

\paragraph{Scope and future directions.}
Our primary analysis is grounded in a single interactive environment with a specific failure mode (the $-100$ death penalty), though the cross-environment evaluations on MATH ($N{=}300$) and ALFWorld ($N{=}135$) provide initial evidence that the counterfactual methodology generalizes: the directional routing trend on MATH and the information-asymmetry pattern on ALFWorld both demonstrate the framework's ability to identify environment-specific routing mechanisms. Extending to environments with graded failure costs, embodied agents with continuous action spaces, or multi-turn dialogue systems would further clarify how broadly death avoidance---or analogous catastrophic-failure avoidance---accounts for apparent routing signal. Step-level counterfactual labeling, rather than episode-level, is a complementary direction that could reveal finer-grained routing structure masked by our current aggregation. A further limitation is the gap between the implicit routing signal we measure---based on episode outcomes---and the representations an implicit router would use at inference time. Our router achieves above-chance step-level accuracy ($54.1\%$) but fails to generalize to held-out episodes ($47.1\%$), and data scaling ablations show this is not resolved by more data---the limitation is structural when always-deterministic is trivially near-optimal. Whether environments with more balanced routing signal (lower DAF) would yield learnable step-level routing remains an open question. Finally, validating these findings across model scales and families beyond 7--8B parameters would establish whether the death-avoidance dominance we observe is a property of the environment or of the capability regime.

