\usepackage{amsmath}
\usepackage{amssymb}
\usepackage{algorithm}
\usepackage{algorithmic}

\title{Death Avoidance as the Dominant Routing Signal:\\A Counterfactual Analysis on ScienceWorld}

\author{Anonymous}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\input{abstract}
\input{introduction}
\input{method}
\input{experiments}
\input{related_work}
\input{discussion}
\input{conclusion}

\section*{Limitations}

Our analysis uses a single benchmark (ScienceWorld) with a single LLM backbone (Qwen2.5-7B-Instruct), and generalization to other interactive environments and model scales remains to be established. A preliminary cross-model pilot with Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct ($N{=}10$ episode pairs, 2 task types) shows directionally consistent death patterns (\S\ref{sec:cross-model}), but full-scale multi-model validation remains future work. The $-100$ death penalty may amplify DAF; environments with less catastrophic penalties could exhibit lower DAF, though the qualitative finding may still hold. On the 135 episodes (55.1\%) where neither strategy leads to catastrophic failure, deterministic computation retains a small advantage ($\Delta_{\text{nd}} = +2.84$), indicating non-zero routing signal beyond death avoidance. Our analysis covers 11 of 30 ScienceWorld task types; the remaining 19 (63\%) were excluded during prescreening because they lack routing signal---53\% show $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = 0$, and both strategies typically time out within the 30-step budget (Appendix~\ref{app:per-task}). These tasks likely require longer-horizon planning; increasing the step budget could surface additional routing signal whose composition may differ from the death-dominated pattern observed here. Stronger models (e.g., 70B-scale or reasoning-specialized) likely exhibit lower internal-strategy death rates, which would reduce DAF. Our finding may reflect a capability-dependent failure mode: at higher capability levels, death avoidance may no longer dominate routing signal.

\paragraph{Cross-environment scope.}
Our MATH and ALFWorld analyses (\S\ref{sec:discussion}) provide cross-environment evidence but do not conclusively establish generalization. In MATH (no death penalty), the deterministic advantage vanishes with a directional trend favoring internal reasoning ($+4.3$\,pp, $p = 0.079$, not reaching $\alpha{=}0.05$). In ALFWorld, the deterministic advantage stems from an information gap (access to admissible actions) rather than death avoidance; moreover, 71.1\% of ALFWorld episodes result in both-strategy failure, limiting the counterfactual signal available from a 7B model on household tasks. Environments with graded failure costs or intermediate penalty regimes remain unstudied.

\paragraph{Reasoning strategy scope.}
Our internal-reasoning baseline uses single-pass ReAct \citep{react-2023}, which lacks self-correction or backtracking. More sophisticated reasoning strategies---such as Reflexion-style self-correction \citep{reflexion-2023} or Tree-of-Thought backtracking \citep{tot-2023}---may reduce the internal strategy's death rate, which would lower DAF. Our findings may therefore be partly specific to the vulnerability of single-pass reasoning rather than a universal property of internal computation.

\paragraph{Step-level mislabeling.} Episode-level labels may assign suboptimal routing decisions to individual steps; in particular, steps preceding a death event are labeled det-preferred even if the death was not foreseeable from those steps' observations. Training on pure-strategy trajectories but deploying on mixed policy also introduces a distribution shift \citep{dagger-2011}.

\paragraph{Scope.}
Extending the counterfactual methodology to environments with graded failure costs, embodied agents, or multi-turn dialogue systems would clarify how broadly death avoidance accounts for apparent routing signal. Step-level labeling with denser rewards and the logit--generation gap in the implicit router are natural next steps. We audit labeling quality in Appendix~\ref{app:labeling}.

\section*{Ethics Statement}

This work uses ScienceWorld \cite{scienceworld-2022}, a publicly available text-based simulation environment, and does not involve human subjects or private data. All language models are accessed via standard APIs or publicly released weights. We report computational costs: approximately 40 GPU-hours on two consumer GPUs for data collection, router training, and evaluation. Code and data will be released upon acceptance.

\bibliography{references}

\appendix

\section{Labeling Quality Audit}
\label{app:labeling}

A potential concern is oracle circularity: whether episode-level labels reflect tie-breaking artifacts rather than genuine modality differences. We audit all 245 labels. Of the 150 \texttt{no\_preference} labels (61.2\%), 115 (46.9\%) are both-alive ties and 35 (14.3\%) are both-dead---all excluded from router training. The remaining 95 training labels are entirely genuine: 75 from death asymmetry (70 deterministic-preferred, 5 internal-preferred) and 20 from score comparison with both strategies surviving (16 deterministic-preferred, 4 internal-preferred). Zero labels depend on tie-breaking. Retraining the router using only death-asymmetry labels yields step-level balanced accuracy of 57.3\%, within 1$\sigma$ of the full-label retrain (57.6 $\pm$ 0.5\%, step-level Trainer evaluation), confirming that score-comparison labels contribute negligible additional signal. (Step-level metrics are used here for the relative comparison; as shown in \S\ref{sec:router-diagnostic}, absolute generalization to new episodes is near chance.) The 97.4\% step-level tie rate (\S\ref{sec:collection}) further motivates episode-level over step-level labeling.

\section{Per-Task Decomposition}
\label{app:per-task}
We select 11 of ScienceWorld's 30 task types via prescreening: task types where both strategies complete at least one episode within the 30-step budget are retained; types where both strategies time out on all seed variations are excluded.\footnote{Prescreening ensures that the counterfactual comparison is meaningful---task types where neither strategy makes progress provide no routing signal.}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\scriptsize
\setlength{\tabcolsep}{2.5pt}
\begin{tabular}{@{}lrrccc@{}}
\toprule
Task & $n$ & $\Delta_\text{full}$ & D\%\textsubscript{int} & D\%\textsubscript{det} & D / I / N \\
\midrule
id-life-stages-1       & 20  & $+47.50$ & 55   & 10   & 9 / 0 / 11  \\
lifespan-long          & 25  & $+44.00$ & 56   & 16   & 11 / 1 / 13 \\
lifespan-l-then-s      & 20  & $+40.80$ & 45   & 10   & 8 / 1 / 11  \\
inclined-plane         & 20  & $+36.00$ & 55   & 20   & 7 / 0 / 13  \\
freeze                 & 20  & $+30.50$ & 55   & 25   & 6 / 0 / 14  \\
meas-melt-point        & 20  & $+30.05$ & 55   & 25   & 6 / 0 / 14  \\
id-life-stages-2       & 20  & $+30.00$ & 50   & 20   & 6 / 0 / 14  \\
find-animal            & 20  & $+26.65$ & 50   & 25   & 5 / 0 / 15  \\
grow-fruit             & 20  & $+25.10$ & 45   & 20   & 6 / 1 / 13  \\
chem-mix               & 20  & $+21.80$ & 45   & 25   & 6 / 2 / 12  \\
\midrule
find-non-living$^\dagger$ & 40 & $+9.58$ & 0  & 0    & 16 / 4 / 20 \\
\midrule
\textbf{All (11 tasks)} & \textbf{245} & $\mathbf{+29.60}$ & \textbf{42.9} & \textbf{16.3} & \textbf{86 / 9 / 150} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Per-task decomposition of the death avoidance effect. D\%: death rate for internal (int) and deterministic (det) strategies. D/I/N: episode labels (det-preferred / internal-preferred / no-preference). Sorted by $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ descending. $\dagger$\,Only task with zero deaths under both strategies; $n{=}40$ because data was collected in two batches, with the second batch adding 20 episodes to increase statistical power for this zero-death outlier.}
\label{tab:per-task}
\end{table}

\paragraph{Task selection and anti-cherry-picking.}
The 11 included task types are those exhibiting meaningful routing signal in prescreening, not those with the highest death rates. The deterministic-strategy death rate is comparable between included (17.8\%) and excluded (14.9\%) task types, confirming that prescreening does not select for death-prone tasks. Rather, the key distinction is the \emph{presence} of routing signal: all 11 included tasks have $\Delta_{\text{nd}} \neq 0$ (non-zero performance gap on surviving episodes), while 53\% of excluded tasks show $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = 0$---neither strategy outperforms the other even when both survive, typically because both time out before making progress. \texttt{find-non-living-thing} serves as an internal counterexample: it has zero deaths under both strategies yet is included because it exhibits non-death routing signal ($\Delta_{\text{full}} = +9.58$ entirely from score differences), and deterministic is not always preferred (4 of 20 non-tie labels favor internal).

\section{Correlation Robustness}
\label{app:correlation}

The Pearson $r = 0.9698$ reported in \S\ref{sec:death-decomp} is robust across multiple diagnostics. Spearman rank correlation is $\rho = 0.986$ ($p = 2.4 \times 10^{-8}$), confirming that the relationship is not driven by outliers or non-linearity. A nonparametric bootstrap (10{,}000 resamples) yields 95\% CI $[0.952, 0.995]$.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}lr@{}}
\toprule
Task removed & $r_{\text{LOO}}$ \\
\midrule
id-life-stages-1       & 0.963 \\
lifespan-long          & 0.969 \\
lifespan-l-then-s      & 0.976 \\
inclined-plane         & 0.969 \\
freeze                 & 0.971 \\
meas-melt-point        & 0.972 \\
id-life-stages-2       & 0.972 \\
find-animal            & 0.970 \\
grow-fruit             & 0.973 \\
chem-mix               & 0.969 \\
find-non-living        & 0.980 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Leave-one-out Pearson $r$ for the death rate differential vs.\ $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ correlation. Full $r = 0.970$; LOO range $[0.963, 0.980]$. No single task type is a leverage point.}
\label{tab:loo}
\end{table}

\section{Router Training Details}
\label{app:router-details}

We use Qwen3-1.7B for the router to avoid circular dependency with the Qwen2.5-7B data-generating model.

With gradient accumulation of 1 (effective batch size 4), routing performance is highly sensitive to the evaluation split, suggesting overfitting to specific train/validation partition boundaries. With gradient accumulation of 8 (effective batch size 32), step-level performance stabilizes across splits (${\sim}54\%$ balanced accuracy). All reported results use gradient accumulation 8.

Under step-level evaluation with a fixed data split, cross-seed balanced accuracy ranges from 52.7\% to 58.1\%, but internal recall varies from 51.5\% to 81.8\%, reflecting threshold sensitivity. However, episode-level evaluation reveals that this apparent discrimination does not generalize: balanced accuracy drops to $47.1 \pm 2.2\%$ across 8 cross-split seeds (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}). Deployed end-to-end, the trained router allocates 97.0\% of episodes to deterministic computation with 17.6\% death rate, and misrouting cost (death from internally-routed episodes) exceeds the benefit of correctly routing rare internal-preferred cases.

\paragraph{Evaluation methods.} All results use \emph{generation-based evaluation}: the model generates a routing label as text (\texttt{enable\_thinking=False}), parsed for classification. We evaluate at two granularities: \emph{step-level} (steps from the same episode may appear in both train and validation sets) and \emph{episode-level} (entire episodes held out). Step-level accuracy ($54.1 \pm 5.4\%$) exceeds chance, indicating the router captures some signal, but episode-level accuracy ($47.1 \pm 2.2\%$, below chance) shows this does not generalize to unseen episodes. A data scaling ablation (Appendix~\ref{app:seed-robustness}) rules out data scarcity, pointing instead to a structural limitation: always-deterministic is trivially near-optimal given DAF $= 90.4\%$. Both metrics are reported in the main text; the model-size ablation (\S\ref{sec:ablations}) uses step-level evaluation.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\resizebox{\columnwidth}{!}{%
\begin{tabular}{@{}lcc@{}}
\toprule
Hyperparameter & Classification Head & Implicit Router \\
\midrule
Base model & Qwen3-1.7B & Qwen3-1.7B \\
Architecture & Linear + LoRA & SFT + LoRA \\
LoRA $r$ / $\alpha$ & 16 / 32 & 16 / 32 \\
Learning rate & 2e-5 & 2e-4 \\
Optimizer & AdamW & AdamW \\
Epochs & 3 & 3 \\
Batch size (per device) & 4 & 4 \\
Gradient accumulation & 8 & 8 \\
Warmup ratio & 0.1 & 0.1 \\
Weight decay & 0.01 & 0.01 \\
Max sequence length & 512 & 512 \\
Seeds & \{42\} & \{42, 123, 456, 789\} \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}}
\caption{Router training hyperparameters. Both architectures use the same base model with LoRA adaptation. The classification head degenerates across all configurations; the implicit router results are reported as 4-seed mean$\pm$std.}
\label{tab:hyperparams}
\end{table}

\section{Seed Robustness}
\label{app:seed-robustness}

We assess router generalization at two evaluation granularities: \emph{step-level}, where steps from the same episode may appear in both train and validation sets, and \emph{episode-level}, where entire episodes are held out. Both use cross-split evaluation (seeds $\{42, \ldots, 49\}$, 8 seeds total), where the data partition and training seed both vary. Table~\ref{tab:seed-robustness} reports per-seed results.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}llcc@{}}
\toprule
Eval.\ Granularity & Seed & Bal.\ Acc.\ (\%) \\
\midrule
Step-level & 42 & 57.06 \\
(same-episode & 43 & 63.99 \\
leakage) & 44 & 55.43 \\
 & 45 & 56.14 \\
 & 46 & 55.45 \\
 & 47 & 50.08 \\
 & 48 & 48.03 \\
 & 49 & 59.30 \\
\cmidrule{2-3}
 & Mean$\pm$std & $54.1 \pm 5.4$ \\
\midrule
Episode-level & 42 & 48.1 \\
(held-out & 43 & 47.9 \\
episodes) & 44 & 49.6 \\
 & 45 & 49.4 \\
 & 46 & 48.5 \\
 & 47 & 43.1 \\
 & 48 & 45.4 \\
 & 49 & 44.8 \\
\cmidrule{2-3}
 & Mean$\pm$std & $47.1 \pm 2.2$ \\
\midrule
\multicolumn{2}{@{}l}{Always-det (= majority-vote)} & 50.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Per-seed router performance under cross-split evaluation (8 seeds, generation-based, \texttt{enable\_thinking=False}). Step-level evaluation allows steps from the same episode in both train and validation sets (same-episode leakage). Episode-level evaluation holds out entire episodes; the resulting $47.1 \pm 2.2\%$ is below the 50\% chance baseline. A data scaling ablation (Table~\ref{tab:data-scaling}) confirms this is structural rather than data-limited.}
\label{tab:seed-robustness}
\end{table}

The contrast between step-level ($54.1 \pm 5.4\%$) and episode-level ($47.1 \pm 2.2\%$) evaluation reveals a generalization gap: the step-level signal likely reflects memorization of episode-specific context (enabled by same-episode leakage) rather than extraction of generalizable routing features. At episode level, the router performs below chance across all 8 seeds (range: 43.1\%--49.6\%). A data scaling ablation (Table~\ref{tab:data-scaling}) rules out data scarcity as the cause: scaling from 25\% to 800\% of the original training set ($32{\times}$ range) yields no improvement, converging to the always-deterministic baseline ($50\%$). The failure is structural: with DAF $= 90.4\%$ and all task types deterministic-majority (86:9 label ratio), always-deterministic trivially solves the episode-level routing problem, leaving no learnable signal for a step-level router.

\paragraph{Data scaling ablation.} To test whether the episode-level generalization failure stems from data scarcity, we vary training set size from 25\% to 800\% of the original via paraphrase-based data augmentation (synonym substitution + template variation on step observations), holding the episode-level validation set fixed. Table~\ref{tab:data-scaling} reports the results.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}rccc@{}}
\toprule
Scale & Train samples & Det\% & Ep.-level Bal.\ Acc.\ (\%) \\
\midrule
25\% & 433 & ${\sim}90$ & $49.5 \pm 0.3$ \\
50\% & 867 & ${\sim}90$ & $51.5$ \\
100\% & 1{,}735 & 89.6 & $49.9 \pm 0.1$ \\
200\% & 3{,}470 & 94.2 & $50.0 \pm 0.0$ \\
800\% & 13{,}880 & ${\sim}97.7$ & $51.0 \pm 1.1$ \\
\midrule
Always-det & --- & --- & 50.0 \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Data scaling ablation: episode-level balanced accuracy across training set sizes (25\%--800\% of original, $32{\times}$ range). Internal recall $= 0.0$ at all scales (complete majority-class collapse). Det\%: fraction of deterministic-preferred labels in training data. The 50\% scale is a single seed; all others report mean$\pm$std across seeds.}
\label{tab:data-scaling}
\end{table}

All scales hover at ${\sim}50\%$ episode-level balanced accuracy---equivalent to the always-deterministic baseline---with no monotonic improvement as data increases. This rules out data scarcity as the explanation for the router's episode-level failure and points instead to a structural limitation: when DAF $= 90.4\%$ and all task types are deterministic-majority (86:9 label ratio), the episode-level routing problem is trivially solved by always choosing deterministic, leaving no learnable signal for a step-level router to exploit.

Augmentation disproportionately increases the majority (deterministic) class: Det\% rises from 89.6\% at 100\% to 94.2\% at 200\% and ${\sim}97.7\%$ at 800\%. However, the complete collapse to majority-class prediction (internal recall $= 0.0$) occurs even at the smallest unaugmented scale (25\%, ${\sim}90\%$ det), confirming this is not an augmentation artifact. Balanced accuracy corrects for class imbalance by averaging per-class recall, so the metric itself is not biased by the skew.

Decomposition metrics are stable under bootstrap resampling (1{,}000 resamples of the 245-episode dataset): DAF $= 90.6\% \pm 4.2\%$ (95\% CI $[81.6\%, 98.8\%]$); Pearson $r = 0.975 \pm 0.011$ (95\% CI $[0.952, 0.995]$). The death avoidance dominance finding is robust to both resampling and split perturbation.

\paragraph{Per-task-type evaluation breakdown.} To test whether the router learns transferable routing strategies or merely task-type classification, we evaluate existing checkpoints (8 seeds) separately on each of the 11 task types. Of the 11 task types, 7 have \emph{all-deterministic} labels (no internal-preferred episodes exist), making balanced accuracy undefined---the router has nothing to learn beyond the trivial always-deterministic default. For the remaining 4 task types where both classes are present, balanced accuracy ranges from 50.0\% to 73.0\%, but this apparent signal does not indicate cross-task-type routing transfer: the router is effectively classifying task types (which correlate with death risk) rather than extracting a generalizable routing strategy. This complements the data scaling and capacity scaling ablations: routing signal does not transfer across task types because the signal \emph{is} the task type---whether a given task carries death risk.

\section{Penalty Sensitivity Analysis}
\label{app:penalty}

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}rrr@{}}
\toprule
$P$ & DAF($P$) & 95\% CI \\
\midrule
0 & 7.5\% & [$-36.7$, $83.1$] \\
10 & 50.4\% & [$19.8$, $94.5$] \\
25 & 70.8\% & [$48.5$, $97.1$] \\
50 & 82.6\% & [$67.6$, $98.4$] \\
100\textsuperscript{*} & 90.4\% & [$81.5$, $99.1$] \\
200 & 95.0\% & [$90.0$, $99.5$] \\
500 & 97.9\% & [$95.8$, $99.8$] \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Sensitivity of DAF to death penalty magnitude $P$. DAF$(P) = 1 - \Delta_{\text{nd}} / \Delta_{\text{full}}(P)$, where $\Delta_{\text{full}}(P) \approx \delta \cdot P + \Delta_{\text{nd}}$, $\delta = 0.2653$ is the death-rate differential, and $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = 2.84$ (strict neither-died filtering). Bootstrap 95\% CIs from 1{,}000 resamples. \textsuperscript{*}Default setting.}
\label{tab:penalty-sensitivity}
\end{table}

The non-death score gap $\Delta_{\text{nd}} = 2.84$ is invariant across penalty levels---it depends only on surviving-episode performance, not on $P$. As $P$ increases, $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ grows ($16.33$ at $P{=}50$, $29.60$ at $P{=}100$, $56.13$ at $P{=}200$) while $\Delta_{\text{nd}}$ remains fixed, mechanically increasing DAF.

\paragraph{Mechanism boundary: death specificity.}
The $r = 0.97$ correlation between per-task death-rate differential and $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ is specific to catastrophic death (score $= -100$). Broader failure definitions dissolve the correlation entirely: defining ``failure'' as any non-positive score ($T{=}0$) yields $r = -0.13$ ($p = 0.70$), because non-death episodes show near-zero performance differential between strategies ($\Delta_{\text{nd}} = 2.84$). Table~\ref{tab:penalty-correlation} quantifies this specificity across penalty magnitudes.

\begin{table}[h]
\centering
\small
\begin{tabular}{@{}rccl@{}}
\toprule
$P$ & $r$ & $p$ & Sig.\ ($\alpha{=}0.05$) \\
\midrule
10 & $-0.08$ & $0.813$ & No \\
25 & $0.51$ & $0.108$ & No \\
50 & $0.86$ & $0.0006$ & Yes \\
100\textsuperscript{*} & $0.97$ & $8 \times 10^{-7}$ & Yes \\
200 & $0.99$ & $1 \times 10^{-9}$ & Yes \\
\bottomrule
\end{tabular}
\caption{Pearson $r$ between per-task death-rate differential and $\Delta_{\text{full}}(P)$ across penalty magnitudes. The correlation is non-significant at low penalties and emerges at $P \geq 50$, reflecting the transition from non-death-dominated to death-dominated score gaps. \textsuperscript{*}Default ScienceWorld setting.}
\label{tab:penalty-correlation}
\end{table}

This sharpens our central finding: death is not merely the dominant component of routing signal---it is effectively the \emph{only} component that generates differential routing signal in ScienceWorld. At low penalties, the death contribution is small and $\Delta_{\text{full}}$ reduces to $\Delta_{\text{nd}} \approx 2.84$, which is near-uniform across task types ($\text{mean non-death gap} = -1.19$ excluding the \texttt{find-non-living-thing} outlier); consequently, death-rate differential has no predictive power. As $P$ increases, the death term dominates $\Delta_{\text{full}}$, and the correlation sharpens because death rates \emph{are} the performance gap. The DAF values in Table~\ref{tab:penalty-sensitivity} increase monotonically with $P$, but this is a mathematical consequence of the decomposition ($\text{DAF} = \delta P / (\delta P + \Delta_{\text{nd}})$), not evidence of mechanism robustness.

